Build-time only patch, applied inside the container to the copied sources.

The 1C mobile platform loads an external component on Android differently from
the desktop platform, and the component has to be built for that platform
without touching the shared sources of the project.

1. The mobile platform loads the library with dlopen and then calls the
   JNI_OnLoad entry point it resolves with dlsym. The result of the lookup is
   not checked for NULL, so a library without that export terminates the
   application with SIGSEGV right in the middle of ПодключитьВнешнююКомпоненту.
   The entry point itself is src/jnienv.cpp: it is compiled into the library
   for Android only by the main CMakeLists.txt, and the symbol is exported
   through src/version.script.

2. The build gets a feature level instead of a plain on/off switch:
     kafka  - Kafka only. The fallback for a platform where the schema
              dependencies do not build.
     schema - plus AVRO, Protobuf and Schema Registry. The Schema Registry is
              reached over plain HTTP, the build has no TLS runtime.
   The sources of the schema methods and the members that need the third
   party headers are behind SIMPLEKAFKA_HAS_SCHEMA.

3. currentDateTime() takes the date and time from the wall clock. libstdc++
   defines high_resolution_clock as system_clock, but libc++ defines it as
   steady_clock, whose epoch is not the Unix epoch, so the code did not even
   compile under the NDK.

4. The component is linked against the C++ runtime of the NDK statically and
   needs no shared library besides the Android runtime itself.

